10-06T15:33:01.352-05:00","title":"How to fix a bug in a fix","url":"https://projectzero.google/2026/10/emergency-patching.html","score":3,"flags":0,"comment_count":1,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"freddyb","user_is_author":false,"tags":["security"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/d0cthg","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/d0cthg/how_fix_bug_fix"},{"short_id":"0slgpl","created_at":"2026-10-06T15:16:33.717-05:00","title":"That Time I Worked With a Laptop Thief (2025)","url":"https://blog.pipetogrep.org/2025/07/27/that-time-i-worked-with-a-laptop-thief/","score":50,"flags":0,"comment_count":8,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"jeanthomas","user_is_author":false,"tags":["security"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/0slgpl","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/0slgpl/time_i_worked_with_laptop_thief_2025"},{"short_id":"jwidjn","created_at":"2026-10-06T14:03:48.107-05:00","title":"Inside the Windows I/O Manager: Deep dive into how read I/O requests are initialized","url":"https://winware31.blogspot.com/2026/10/inside-windows-io-manager-deep-dive.html","score":6,"flags":0,"comment_count":1,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"calvin","user_is_author":false,"tags":["reversing","windows"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/jwidjn","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/jwidjn/inside_windows_i_o_manager_deep_dive_into"},{"short_id":"jxhi2i","created_at":"2026-10-06T13:29:11.053-05:00","title":"A sustainable web career, for when all this blows over","url":"https://dbushell.com/2026/10/07/sustainable-web-career/","score":35,"flags":1,"comment_count":18,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"nrvous","user_is_author":false,"tags":["vibecoding","web"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/jxhi2i","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/jxhi2i/sustainable_web_career_for_when_all_this"},{"short_id":"tcmpcw","created_at":"2026-10-06T13:27:16.404-05:00","title":"Pared - remove unwanted Apple Intelligence models without disabling SIP","url":"https://github.com/4evy/pared","score":13,"flags":1,"comment_count":2,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"FlameFlag","user_is_author":true,"tags":["mac","show","vibecoding"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/tcmpcw","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/tcmpcw/pared_remove_unwanted_apple"},{"short_id":"uvpts7","created_at":"2026-10-06T13:11:34.419-05:00","title":"Perseus: A Fail-Slow Detection Framework for Cloud Storage Systems (2023)","url":"https://www.usenix.org/system/files/fast23-lu.pdf","score":1,"flags":0,"comment_count":0,"description":"\u003cp\u003eAbstract: The newly-emerging “fail-slow” failures plague both software and hardware where the victim components are still functioning yet with degraded performance. To address this problem, this paper presents PERSEUS, a practical fail-slow detection framework for storage devices. PERSEUS leverages a light regression-based model to fast pinpoint and analyze fail-slow failures at the granularity of drives. Within a 10-month close monitoring on 248K drives, PERSEUS managed to find 304 fail-slow cases. Isolating them can reduce the (node-level) 99.99th tail latency by 48%. We assemble a large-scale fail-slow dataset (including 41K normal drives and 315 verified fail-slow drives) from our production traces, based on which we provide root cause analysis on fail-slow drives covering a variety of ill-implemented scheduling, hardware defects, and environmental factors. We have released the dataset to the public for fail-slow study.\u003c/p\u003e\n","description_plain":"Abstract: The newly-emerging “fail-slow” failures plague both software and hardware where the victim components are still functioning yet with degraded performance. To address this problem, this paper presents PERSEUS, a practical fail-slow detection framework for storage devices. PERSEUS leverages a light regression-based model to fast pinpoint and analyze fail-slow failures at the granularity of drives. Within a 10-month close monitoring on 248K drives, PERSEUS managed to find 304 fail-slow cases. Isolating them can reduce the (node-level) 99.99th tail latency by 48%. We assemble a large-scale fail-slow dataset (including 41K normal drives and 315 verified fail-slow drives) from our production traces, based on which we provide root cause analysis on fail-slow drives covering a variety of ill-implemented scheduling, hardware defects, and environmental factors. We have released the dataset to the public for fail-slow study.","submitter_user":"typesanitizer","user_is_author":false,"tags":["distributed","pdf","performance"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/uvpts7","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/uvpts7/perseus_fail_slow_detection_framework"},{"short_id":"0xpi8s","created_at":"2026-10-06T13:03:04.730-05:00","title":"Why We Should Inject Dependencies","url":"https://gist.github.com/alassek/1a185a8cdae20ddd991a8cd3cd90d347","score":7,"flags":0,"comment_count":7,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"PuercoPop","user_is_author":false,"tags":["ruby"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/0xpi8s","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/0xpi8s/why_we_should_inject_dependencies"},{"short_id":"4fop73","created_at":"2026-10-06T13:00:33.008-05:00","title":"Chrome's Response to Recent ccTLD Registry Hijacks","url":"https://blog.google/security/chromes-response-to-recent-cctld-registry-hijacks/","score":6,"flags":0,"comment_count":0,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"agwa","user_is_author":false,"tags":["security"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/4fop73","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/4fop73/chrome_s_response_recent_cctld_registry"},{"short_id":"fpcicg","created_at":"2026-10-06T12:46:53.386-05:00","title":"Last rites for Gentoo's Chromium package","url":"https://lwn.net/SubscriberLink/1097760/2be4d9e3eeb59039/","score":30,"flags":0,"comment_count":4,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"calvin",